# 20. SECURITY ENGINEERING

Security is a mandatory development requirement.

Implement a defense-in-depth security architecture.

## Authentication Security

- Secure Supabase authentication.
- Email verification.
- Password reset tokens.
- Secure session handling.
- Session expiration.
- Session revocation where supported.
- Account deactivation.
- Login error handling.
- Brute-force protection.

## Database Security

- Enable RLS on every tenant-sensitive table.
- Create restrictive policies.
- Verify user identity through trusted authenticated context.
- Verify school membership.
- Verify resource ownership.
- Verify role permissions.
- Prevent cross-school data access.
- Restrict service-role access to trusted server-side code.

## API Security

- Validate all incoming data with Zod.
- Reject unauthorized requests.
- Rate-limit sensitive endpoints.
- Protect administrative operations.
- Validate uploaded files.
- Protect against mass assignment.
- Prevent insecure direct object references.
- Handle duplicate requests safely.

## Web Security

Protect against:

- SQL injection.
- Cross-site scripting (XSS).
- Cross-site request forgery (CSRF), where applicable.
- Broken access control.
- IDOR.
- Authentication bypass.
- Privilege escalation.
- Brute-force attacks.
- Malicious uploads.
- Open redirects.
- Sensitive data leakage.

Implement suitable security headers and Content Security Policy.

Never expose:

- Supabase service role key.
- Database passwords.
- Private API keys.
- Signing secrets.
- SMTP credentials.

Never put privileged credentials in NEXT_PUBLIC environment variables.

Treat all browser data as untrusted.

